11. What Metro Organizations Must Get Right Before Deploying AI and IoT
Successful connected safety programs require more than software procurement.

Cybersecurity
Connected systems must use appropriate security controls and protect operational technology environments.

Data Quality
AI outputs depend on the quality, relevance and completeness of available information.

Connectivity
Underground environments may require specific communication arrangements.

Privacy
Connected worker applications should collect only necessary information and follow applicable privacy requirements.

Device Reliability
Monitoring devices need appropriate calibration, maintenance and validation.

Human Accountability
Qualified personnel must remain responsible for safety-critical decisions.

Workforce Adoption
Workers need clear procedures, training and confidence that reported concerns will receive appropriate attention.

Technology should simplify safe work—not create unnecessary administrative burdens.

12. A Practical Roadmap for Metro Safety Digital Transformation
Phase 1 — Digitize essential safety workflows.

Standardize incident reporting, inspections, risk assessments, permits, audits and corrective actions.

Phase 2 — Connect frontline personnel.

Introduce mobile reporting, field-accessible safety information and suitable notifications.

Phase 3 — Integrate relevant monitoring systems.

Evaluate approved IoT, CCTV, asset-management and enterprise integrations.

Phase 4 — Introduce AI-assisted analytics.

Identify recurring deficiencies, risk patterns and unresolved high-risk conditions.

Phase 5 — Build executive safety intelligence.

Provide relevant dashboards across stations, depots, contractors and maintenance activities.

Phase 6 — Measure effectiveness.

Review reporting timeliness, control effectiveness, corrective-action closure and worker participation.

Scale the approach after validating its operational value and safety assurance.

Conclusion: The Future of Metro Safety Is Connected—and Human-Centered
Metro networks will continue to expand.

Their infrastructure will become more sophisticated.

Their maintenance requirements will become more demanding.

And the volume of operational information will continue to grow.

But the central responsibility will remain the same: protecting passengers, workers, contractors and infrastructure.

AI can help identify patterns.

IoT can provide relevant monitoring information.

Connected worker technology can improve communication and field participation.

Digital EHS systems can connect these activities with accountable safety processes.

The greatest opportunity lies in bringing them together.

The future of metro safety will not be defined by the number of technologies deployed, but by how effectively those technologies help people prevent harm.

